# SECTION 7: C++ TYPE CASTING

In C and C++, type casting is the method used to convert a variable from one data type to another. C uses a single, blunt casting style, while C++ introduces four specialized type-safe casting operators to prevent subtle programming bugs.

## 7.1 C-Style Casting
This is the traditional cast used in C. It forces the compiler to convert data types without running strict safety checks.
```c
int a = 10;
double b = (double)a; // Explicit C-style cast
```

## 7.2 C++ Named Casts
C++ introduces four specific casting operators. They tell the compiler exactly why you are casting, making your code safer and much easier to search for during code reviews.

**static_cast** — go-to cast for safe, well-defined conversions at compile time. Handles standard conversions like turning an integer into a float, or converting pointers up and down an inheritance hierarchy.
```cpp
float f = 3.14;
int i = static_cast<int>(f); // Compiles cleanly, truncates to 3
```

**dynamic_cast** — used exclusively for handling inheritance hierarchies at runtime. Safely converts a base class pointer or reference to a derived class pointer or reference. Relies on RTTI, meaning it only works if your base class has at least one virtual function.
- Best use case: Safe downcasting. If the cast is impossible, a pointer cast returns nullptr, while a reference cast throws a std::bad_cast exception.
```cpp
Base* b = new Derived();
Derived* d = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b);
if (d) {
    // Cast succeeded! Safe to use Derived methods.
}
```

**const_cast** — the only cast in C++ that can add or remove the const modifier from a variable.
- Best use case: Passing a constant variable into an older, third-party legacy function that accepts a non-const pointer but doesn't actually alter the underlying data.
```cpp
void legacy_function(char* str);
const char* text = "Hello";
legacy_function(const_cast<char*>(text)); // Drops const safety
```

**reinterpret_cast** — low-level, high-risk cast that instructs the compiler to treat the underlying raw binary bit pattern of an object as an entirely different type. Zero structural or safety checks.
```cpp
int* p = new int(65);
char* ch = reinterpret_cast<char*>(p); // Treats the int bits as a char buffer
```

**Quick Comparison Matrix**
| Cast Type | When it Happens | Safety Level | Primary Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| static_cast | Compile-time | High | Standard implicit conversions & safe up/down casts. |
| dynamic_cast | Run-time | High | Safe navigation of polymorphic class structures. |
| const_cast | Compile-time | Medium | Stripping away or adding const or volatile properties. |
| reinterpret_cast | Compile-time | Low | Raw binary conversions (e.g., matching pointers with hardware addresses). |

