# SECTION 8: C++ OOP FOUR PILLARS

OOP is a programming paradigm centered around objects (data) rather than actions (logic). C++ natively supports OOP, whereas pure C does not (though you can mimic it using structures and function pointers).

## 8.1 Encapsulation (Data Hiding)
Encapsulation is the process of bundling data (variables) and the methods (functions) that operate on that data into a single unit called a Class. It restricts direct access to some of an object's components to prevent accidental corruption.
- Access Specifiers: Controls the visibility of class members.
  - public: Accessible from anywhere outside the class.
  - private: Accessible only from within the class (achieves data hiding).
  - protected: Accessible within the class and its derived (child) classes.
- Getters and Setters: Public methods used to safely read and modify private variables, allowing you to validate data before updating it.

## 8.2 Abstraction (Hiding Complexity)
Abstraction means hiding internal implementation details and showing only the essential features to the outside world. While Encapsulation hides data, Abstraction hides complexity (the "how").
- Abstract Class: A class that cannot be instantiated directly and serves as a blueprint for other classes. In C++, a class becomes abstract if it contains at least one Pure Virtual Function.
- Pure Virtual Function: A function with no implementation in the base class, forced to be overridden by derived classes. Written as: `virtual void display() = 0;`.
- Interfaces: A class containing only pure virtual functions and no data members. It dictates a strict contract of what a derived class must implement.

## 8.3 Inheritance (Code Reusability)
Inheritance allows a new class (Derived/Child Class) to acquire the properties and behaviors of an existing class (Base/Parent Class). This establishes an "Is-A" relationship (e.g., a Dog is a Animal).
- Types of Inheritance:
  - Single: One child inherits from one parent.
  - Multiple: One child inherits from more than one parent class (supported in C++, but can lead to the Diamond Problem).
  - Multilevel: A chain of inheritance (Grandparent → Parent → Child).
  - Hierarchical: One parent class has multiple child classes.
  - Hybrid: A combination of multiple and multilevel inheritance.
- The Diamond Problem & Virtual Inheritance: If Class B and C inherit from A, and Class D inherits from both B and C, Class D gets two copies of Class A's members. C++ solves this ambiguity using virtual inheritance (`class B : virtual public A`).

## 8.4 Polymorphism (Many Forms)
Polymorphism allows a single interface to execute different underlying logic depending on how it is invoked. Split into two types:

**A. Compile-Time Polymorphism (Static Binding)** — compiler determines which function to call during compilation.
- Function Overloading: Multiple functions in the same scope have the same name but different parameters.
- Operator Overloading: Giving special meaning to standard operators (like +, -, <<) when used with user-defined object types.

**B. Runtime Polymorphism (Dynamic Binding)** — the exact function to call is determined at execution time based on the actual object type, not the pointer type.
- Function Overriding: A child class provides a specific implementation for a function that is already defined in its parent class.
- Virtual Functions & Vtables: To enable overriding, the base class function must be marked with the virtual keyword.
  - Vtable (Virtual Table): A hidden static array created by the compiler for every class containing virtual functions. It holds pointers to the virtual functions.
  - Vptr (Virtual Pointer): A hidden pointer added to every object instance that points to the class's Vtable, resolving function calls at runtime.

## 8.5 Essential Structural Subtopics
- Class vs. Object: A Class is a user-defined data type or blueprint; an Object is a physical instance of that class allocated in memory.
- Constructors: Special member functions called automatically when an object is created.
  - Default Constructor: Takes no arguments.
  - Parameterized Constructor: Takes arguments to initialize fields.
  - Copy Constructor: Initializes an object using another existing object of the same class (`ClassName(const ClassName &obj)`).
- Destructors: Called automatically when an object goes out of scope or is deleted.
  - Critical Interview Concept: Always make your base class destructor virtual (`virtual ~Base()`). If you don't, deleting a derived class object through a base pointer will cause a memory leak, as the derived class destructor won't be called.
- Composition vs. Inheritance: Inheritance represents an "Is-A" relationship, while Composition represents a "Has-A" relationship (e.g., a Car has an Engine object inside it). Senior developers generally prefer composition over inheritance for flexibility.

## 8.6 Diamond Problem — full working code
The Diamond Problem occurs in multiple inheritance when a class inherits from two classes that both share a common grandparent. Without virtual inheritance, the grandparent's members are duplicated, causing compiler ambiguity and memory waste.
```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Grandparent Class
class Device {
public:
    int power_rating;
    Device() : power_rating(100) {
        std::cout << "Device (Grandparent) Constructor Called\n";
    }
    virtual ~Device() = default;
};

// Parent 1 - Inherits virtually
class Scanner : virtual public Device {
public:
    Scanner() {
        std::cout << "Scanner (Parent 1) Constructor Called\n";
    }
};

// Parent 2 - Inherits virtually
class Printer : virtual public Device {
public:
    Printer() {
        std::cout << "Printer (Parent 2) Constructor Called\n";
    }
};

// Child Class - Inherits from both Parents
class AllInOnePrinter : public Scanner, public Printer {
public:
    AllInOnePrinter() {
        std::cout << "AllInOnePrinter (Child) Constructor Called\n";
    }

    void printPower() {
        // Without 'virtual' above, this next line would fail to compile!
        // The compiler wouldn't know whether to access Scanner::power_rating or Printer::power_rating.
        std::cout << "Current Power Rating: " << power_rating << "W\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    std::cout << "--- Creating AllInOnePrinter Object ---\n";
    AllInOnePrinter machine;

    std::cout << "\n--- Accessing Inherited Member ---\n";
    machine.printPower();

    // Verifying memory allocation efficiency
    std::cout << "\nSize of AllInOnePrinter object: " << sizeof(machine) << " bytes\n";
    return 0;
}
```

