# SECTION 10: SHALLOW COPY VS. DEEP COPY

## 10.1 Conceptual Breakdown
The difference between a Shallow Copy and a Deep Copy comes down to how memory addresses (pointers) are handled during data duplication.
- Shallow Copy: Copies all member field values directly from one object to another. If an object contains a pointer to heap memory, only the memory address is copied. Both the original and copied objects now point to the exact same memory location.
  - The Danger: If one object modifies the data, it changes for both. When one object goes out of scope, it frees the memory, leaving the other object with a dangling pointer. When the second object is destroyed, it attempts to free the same memory address again, triggering a fatal double-free runtime crash.
- Deep Copy: Allocates a brand-new, independent block of memory on the heap for the destination object, then copies the actual content over. Modifying or destroying one object has zero impact on the other.

## 10.2 Implementation Syntax (C++)
By default, the C++ compiler automatically generates a shallow copy constructor and assignment operator. To achieve a deep copy, you must explicitly define them.
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstring>

class SecurityToken {
private:
    char* tokenID;

public:
    // Standard Constructor
    SecurityToken(const char* id) {
        tokenID = new char[strlen(id) + 1];
        strcpy(tokenID, id);
    }

    // Destructor
    ~SecurityToken() {
        delete[] tokenID;
    }

    // DEEP COPY CONSTRUCTOR SYNTAX
    SecurityToken(const SecurityToken& other) {
        // 1. Allocate completely new heap memory
        tokenID = new char[strlen(other.tokenID) + 1];
        // 2. Copy the actual data inside the memory block
        strcpy(tokenID, other.tokenID);
    }

    // DEEP COPY ASSIGNMENT OPERATOR SYNTAX
    SecurityToken& operator=(const SecurityToken& other) {
        // 1. Guard against self-assignment (e.g., t1 = t1)
        if (this != &other) {
            // 2. Clear out our own existing heap memory to prevent a memory leak
            delete[] tokenID;
            // 3. Allocate new memory matching the size of the source object
            tokenID = new char[strlen(other.tokenID) + 1];
            // 4. Copy the raw contents over
            strcpy(tokenID, other.tokenID);
        }
        return *this; // Return reference to allow chaining (e.g., t3 = t2 = t1)
    }
};
```

## 10.3 Comparison Matrix
| Attribute | Shallow Copy (Compiler Default) | Deep Copy (User-Defined) |
|---|---|---|
| Memory Allocation | Does not allocate new heap memory. | Allocates a new distinct block on the heap. |
| Pointer Behavior | Copies only the raw address pointer. | Copies the actual data the pointer references. |
| Independence | Objects are dependent on each other. | Objects are completely isolated. |
| Performance Cost | Extremely fast (O(1) complexity). | Slower (O(N) complexity) due to memory allocation and data copying. |
| Common Crash Risk | High risk of double-free errors or dangling pointers. | Safe from cross-object memory corruption. |

**Crucial Interview Questions on this Topic**
- Q: If Shallow Copy is fast and Deep Copy is safe but slow, how does modern C++ balance speed and safety when returning objects from functions?
  Answer: Modern C++ uses Move Semantics (Rule of 5). Instead of performing a slow deep copy of a temporary object's data, the compiler executes a shallow copy of the resource pointer and then sets the temporary object's pointer to nullptr. This acts as a blazing-fast "ownership transfer" without risking a double-free crash.
- Q: How do you implement a Shallow Copy vs. Deep Copy concept in pure C?
  Answer: In C, a shallow copy is simply a structure assignment (`struct1 = struct2;`). If the structure has pointers, they will copy blindly. To implement a deep copy, you must pass the destination structure pointer to a dedicated utility function that manually uses malloc() and memcpy() for every internal pointer.

