# SECTION 11: RULE OF 5 (Copy/Move Constructors & Assignment) — all three of your original code examples, kept

## 11.1 Concept
- Copy Constructor & Copy Assignment Operator: A copy constructor creates a new object as an exact copy of an existing object. A copy assignment operator (=) copies the contents of an already existing object into another already existing object.
  - Deep Copy vs. Shallow Copy: If a class manages raw heap memory, a shallow copy (default bitwise copy) just copies the pointer address, leading to multiple objects freeing the same memory (double-free corruption). A deep copy allocates new memory and copies the actual data.
- Move Constructor & Move Assignment Operator (C++11): A move constructor "steals" resources from a temporary object (rvalue) rather than allocating new memory and copying data. Instead of duplicating a large block of heap memory, it simply copies the pointer address, sets the temporary object's pointer to nullptr, and avoids expensive memory allocation. This drastically improves performance.

## 11.2 Rule of 5 Syntax
```cpp
class MyClass {
private:
    int* data; // Dynamically allocated resource

public:
    // 1. Destructor
    ~MyClass();

    // 2. Copy Constructor
    MyClass(const MyClass& other);

    // 3. Copy Assignment Operator
    MyClass& operator=(const MyClass& other);

    // 4. Move Constructor (C++11)
    MyClass(MyClass&& other) noexcept;

    // 5. Move Assignment Operator (C++11)
    MyClass& operator=(MyClass&& other) noexcept;
};
```

## 11.3 Example 1 — SimpleBuffer (int array)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstring>

class SimpleBuffer {
private:
    size_t size;
    int* data;

public:
    // 1. Constructor
    SimpleBuffer(size_t s) : size(s), data(new int[s]) {
        std::cout << "Constructor: Allocated " << size << " ints.\n";
    }

    // 2. Destructor
    ~SimpleBuffer() {
        delete[] data;
        std::cout << "Destructor: Freed memory.\n";
    }

    // 3. Copy Constructor (Deep Copy)
    SimpleBuffer(const SimpleBuffer& other) : size(other.size), data(new int[other.size]) {
        std::copy(other.data, other.data + other.size, data);
        std::cout << "Copy Constructor: Deep copied data.\n";
    }

    // 4. Copy Assignment Operator
    SimpleBuffer& operator=(const SimpleBuffer& other) {
        if (this == &other) return *this; // Handle self-assignment

        int* newData = new int[other.size]; // Allocate first to ensure strong exception safety
        std::copy(other.data, other.data + other.size, newData);

        delete[] data; // Free old resource
        data = newData;
        size = other.size;

        std::cout << "Copy Assignment: Copied data.\n";
        return *this;
    }

    // 5. Move Constructor (Steals resources)
    SimpleBuffer(SimpleBuffer&& other) noexcept : size(other.size), data(other.data) {
        other.size = 0;
        other.data = nullptr; // Nullify source pointer
        std::cout << "Move Constructor: Stole resources.\n";
    }

    // 6. Move Assignment Operator
    SimpleBuffer& operator=(SimpleBuffer&& other) noexcept {
        if (this == &other) return *this;

        delete[] data; // Free current resource

        // Steal
        size = other.size;
        data = other.data;

        // Nullify source
        other.size = 0;
        other.data = nullptr;

        std::cout << "Move Assignment: Stole resources.\n";
        return *this;
    }

    void print() const {
        std::cout << "Buffer size: " << size << ", Data address: " << data << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    SimpleBuffer buf1(1000);             // Calls standard constructor
    SimpleBuffer buf2 = buf1;            // Calls Copy Constructor (Deep copy)

    SimpleBuffer buf3(500);              // Calls standard constructor
    buf3 = std::move(buf1);              // Calls Move Assignment Operator

    SimpleBuffer buf4 = std::move(buf2); // Calls Move Constructor

    return 0;
}
