## 11.5 Example 3 — Buffer (with noexcept discussion)
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

class Buffer {
private:
    int* data;
    size_t size;

public:
    // Regular Constructor
    Buffer(size_t s) : size(s), data(new int[s]) {
        std::cout << "Allocated buffer of size " << size << "\n";
    }

    // 1. Destructor
    ~Buffer() {
        delete[] data;
        std::cout << "Destroyed buffer, memory freed\n";
    }

    // 2. Copy Constructor (Deep Copy)
    Buffer(const Buffer& other) : size(other.size), data(new int[other.size]) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
            data[i] = other.data[i];
        }
        std::cout << "Copy Constructor: Deep copy completed\n";
    }

    // 3. Copy Assignment Operator (Deep Copy with cleanup)
    Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& other) {
        std::cout << "Copy Assignment Operator called\n";
        if (this != &other) { // Guard against self-assignment (e.g., b1 = b1)
            delete[] data;    // Free existing resource

            size = other.size;
            data = new int[size];
            for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
                data[i] = other.data[i];
            }
        }
        return *this;
    }

    // 4. Move Constructor (Shallow Copy + Stealing Resources)
    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept : data(other.data), size(other.size) {
        std::cout << "Move Constructor: Resource stolen!\n";

        // CRUCIAL STEP: Nullify the original pointer so its destructor doesn't delete our stolen memory
        other.data = nullptr;
        other.size = 0;
    }

    // 5. Move Assignment Operator (Clean existing + Steal resources)
    Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept {
        std::cout << "Move Assignment Operator called\n";
        if (this != &other) { // Guard against self-assignment
            delete[] data;    // Free our existing resource

            data = other.data; // Steal the source data pointer
            size = other.size;

            other.data = nullptr; // Nullify source to avoid double-free
            other.size = 0;
        }
        return *this;
    }
};

int main() {
    std::cout << "--- Creating b1 ---\n";
    Buffer b1(100);

    std::cout << "\n--- Copying b1 to b2 (Copy Constructor) ---\n";
    Buffer b2 = b1; // Performs slow deep copy of 100 elements

    std::cout << "\n--- Moving b1 to b3 (Move Constructor) ---\n";
    // std::move casts b1 to an rvalue reference, telling the compiler it's safe to steal its data
    Buffer b3 = std::move(b1);

    std::cout << "\n--- Exiting Program ---\n";
    return 0;
}
```

**Crucial Interview Questions on this Code**
- Q: Why is noexcept critical for Move Constructors/Assignment operators?
  Answer: If a move constructor throws an exception midway, the object state becomes corrupt. Standard library containers like std::vector will default back to copying elements during a resize unless your move operations are explicitly marked noexcept. For high-performance backend systems, omitting noexcept causes massive performance bottlenecks.
- Q: What happens if you forget to set other.data = nullptr inside the move constructor?
  Answer: A fatal double-free runtime error occurs. When the temporary/old object (other) goes out of scope, its destructor (`delete[] data`) executes and frees the exact same memory pointer that your new object just stole.

