# SECTION 12: SMART POINTERS

## 12.1 Syntax
Smart pointers are included in the `<memory>` header. They automatically free heap memory when they go out of scope, preventing memory leaks.
- `std::unique_ptr` (Exclusive Ownership) — Syntax: `std::unique_ptr<Type> ptr = std::make_unique<Type>(args);` Key Rule: Cannot be copied. It can only be moved using std::move().
- `std::shared_ptr` (Shared Ownership) — Syntax: `std::shared_ptr<Type> ptr = std::make_shared<Type>(args);` Key Rule: Keeps a reference count. Memory is deleted only when the last shared_ptr pointing to it is destroyed.
- `std::weak_ptr` (Non-owning Observer) — Syntax: `std::weak_ptr<Type> wptr = shared_ptr_instance;` Key Rule: Does not increase the reference count. Used to prevent circular dependencies. To access the data, you must temporarily convert it back to a shared_ptr using `.lock()`.

## 12.2 Concise Code Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

class Resource {
public:
    Resource() { std::cout << "Resource Created\n"; }
    ~Resource() { std::cout << "Resource Destroyed\n"; }
    void sayHello() { std::cout << "Hello from Resource!\n"; }
};

int main() {
    std::cout << "--- 1. Unique Pointer ---\n";
    {
        // Allocation
        std::unique_ptr<Resource> uptr1 = std::make_unique<Resource>();

        // std::unique_ptr<Resource> uptr2 = uptr1; // ERROR: Copying is banned!
        std::unique_ptr<Resource> uptr2 = std::move(uptr1); // Valid: Ownership transferred
    } // uptr2 goes out of scope here -> Resource is automatically Destroyed

    std::cout << "\n--- 2. Shared & Weak Pointer ---\n";
    std::weak_ptr<Resource> wptr;
    {
        // Allocation
        std::shared_ptr<Resource> sptr1 = std::make_shared<Resource>();
        std::cout << "Reference Count: " << sptr1.use_count() << "\n"; // Prints 1

        {
            std::shared_ptr<Resource> sptr2 = sptr1; // Valid: Ownership shared
            std::cout << "Reference Count: " << sptr1.use_count() << "\n"; // Prints 2

            wptr = sptr1; // Weak pointer observes, does NOT increase count
            std::cout << "Reference Count (with weak): " << sptr1.use_count() << "\n"; // Still 2
        } // sptr2 goes out of scope -> Reference count drops to 1

        std::cout << "Reference Count: " << sptr1.use_count() << "\n"; // Prints 1
    } // sptr1 goes out of scope -> Count becomes 0 -> Resource is automatically Destroyed

    std::cout << "\n--- 3. Checking Weak Pointer Validity ---\n";
    if (wptr.expired()) {
        std::cout << "Resource no longer exists! Weak pointer safely detected expiration.\n";
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Crucial Interview Question on this Code**
- Q: Why should you prefer std::make_shared over `std::shared_ptr<Resource>(new Resource())`?
  Answer: Performance and memory optimization. std::make_shared performs a single heap allocation for both the object itself and its internal control block (which stores the reference count). Using new forces two separate heap allocations, which increases memory fragmentation and tracking overhead.

